{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>For they are not like the women, etc.</b> One must examine why the verse extended so at length, when it would have sufficed to say ״the Hebrew women are not like the Egyptian women״ or ״the Hebrew women are not as the Egyptian women.״ Further, one must examine what answer they gave with their mouths regarding the suspicion of supplying food, and it appears to me that Pharaoh accepted their reply. Further, it is difficult that it says <b>they have given birth</b>, when it should have said ״are giving birth.״" "<b>Indeed,</b> the intention of the verse is that they answered him not to take a measure for the Hebrew women from the exceptional ones among the Egyptian women, for all of them are wiser than all the Egyptian women — and this is what it says <b>for they are lively</b>, and they deliver one another, or else they do not need a midwife." "Or perhaps they alluded that the women of the children of Israel possessed two kinds of discernment: one, that they were Hebrew from their very root, and the second, that they were now Egyptian, and through this twofold means they were wise in both kinds of discernment. And this is the measure of the verse: ״for they are not like the women״ commonly found — these are they who are Egyptian Hebrews, and by this there is no contradiction either from Hebrews or from Egyptians, for these possess both qualities — understand this. And the verse's saying <b>before</b> etc. was intended to answer if one should say: and why could they not serve as their midwives against their will, since they are the ones appointed by the king? — and they said ״before״ etc., meaning: for we contrive to come to them before the time of birth arrives according to their reckoning, in order to deliver them, and even so we find them already delivered, for the women giving birth would cleverly extend the time of their labor, for they sensed the matter, and all this is included in the word ״lively.״ From now on, according to this line of reasoning, the suspicion vanishes on its own: even though they would bring in their hands water and food, it would be supposed that they had not yet given birth and were coming to deliver them, and whatever they did out of a show of fondness for them was on the contrary entirely proper and fitting, in order to draw close the hearts of the women in labor so that they would not be suspected in their eyes as murderers, and they would find opportunity to fulfill the king's decree to put the sons to death. And since they would bring them to think they had not yet given birth as mentioned, and would find that they had already given birth — this is not subject to the accusation brought, and with this the complaint and suspicion of Pharaoh was utterly swept away, even though the words of those who go about as talebearers who said that the midwives supplied food were correct."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:23:24.142242050Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}